srow(41, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND IT SHALL BE, IF YOU HEARKEN DILIGENTLY TO MY COMMANDMENTS" (11:13) — WHY IS IT SAID? BECAUSE IT IS SAID "AND YOU SHALL LEARN THEM AND KEEP TO DO THEM" (5:1), I MIGHT HEAR THAT THEY WERE NOT BOUND TO STUDY UNTIL THEY WERE BOUND TO THE DEED; THE TEACHING SAYS "IF YOU HEARKEN DILIGENTLY TO MY COMMANDMENTS" — IT TELLS THAT THEY WERE BOUND TO STUDY AT ONCE. The head row of piska 41 (on 11:13; {SP_(41, 1, "שמיד", "נתחיבו", "בתלמוד")} "that they were bound to study at once"): 5:1\'s ORDER — "learn them" before "keep to do them" — raised as a doubt (does the duty to learn wait on the duty to do?) and closed by 11:13\'s "hearken" read as study standing on its own; the row cites 5:1 in both files ("(דברים ה א)" — "Deuteronomy 5:1" — in the Hebrew, "(Dt.5:1)" in the English; the export\'s verse the DB\'s here, before the division parts). The middah: the doubt raised on one verse and closed by a second (I2\'s form — a REFERENCE the Sifrei draws by the verb "hearken", LR1); the English\'s footnote sends to Tosefta Menachot 6:20. Fresh in both files.')
srow(41, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL LEARN THEM AND KEEP TO DO THEM" (5:1) — IT TELLS THAT THE DEED DEPENDS ON THE STUDY, AND THE STUDY DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE DEED; AND SO WE FIND THAT HE PUNISHED FOR THE STUDY MORE THAN FOR THE DEED, AS IT IS SAID "HEAR THE WORD OF THE LORD, HOUSE OF ISRAEL, FOR THE LORD HAS A QUARREL WITH THE INHABITANTS OF THE LAND, FOR THERE IS NO TRUTH AND NO KINDNESS AND NO KNOWLEDGE OF GOD IN THE LAND" (HOSEA 4:1). The continuation row ({SP_(41, 4, "שמעשה", "תלוי", "בתלמוד")} "that the deed depends on the study"): THE EXPORT\'S FORM — the Hebrew quotes 5:1 whole with NO citation in parentheses (the scan\'s Hebrew regex finds nothing; Hosea\'s "(הושע ד א)" — "Hosea 4:1" — its one cite), the English alone cites "(Dt.5:1)": a row found by the English file (asserted); 5:1\'s "learn … and keep to do" read as a dependency — the deed hangs on the study, not the study on the deed — and the punishment weighed by Hosea\'s three absences (truth, kindness, KNOWLEDGE). The middah: the order of two verbs in one verse read as a hierarchy (E12\'s kin — the sequence of the ink as its logic), with the prophet\'s verse as the proof of weight (a REFERENCE by the word "knowledge", the Sifrei\'s own, LR1). Fresh in both files.')
srow(233, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"YOU SHALL NOT WEAR A MINGLED STUFF … YOU SHALL MAKE YOURSELF FRINGES" (22:11-12) — BOTH WERE SAID IN ONE UTTERANCE; "REMEMBER" (EXODUS 20:8) AND "KEEP" (5:12) — BOTH WERE SAID IN ONE UTTERANCE. The head row of piska 233 (on 22:11-12; {SP_(233, 1, "שניהם", "נאמרו", "בדבור", "אחד")} "both were said in one utterance" — twice in the row, computed): THE SHELF\'S OWN READING OF THE DIFF\'S FIRST WORD — 5:12\'s "keep" against Exodus 20:8\'s "remember" (the two infinitive absolutes, the morphology measured: "keep" so at 5:12, 6:17, 16:1; "remember" so at 20:8 and five more) declared ONE UTTERANCE, the pair set beside the mingled stuff and the fringes (the one forbids, the other commands the same wool-and-linen: the pair a contradiction spoken as one); the row cites both copies — "(שמות כ ח)" ("Exodus 20:8") and "(דברים ה יב)" ("Deuteronomy 5:12") — the export\'s verse the DB\'s (the division parts at 17). The English\'s footnote names the Mekhilta, Bahodesh 7 — the FIRST COPY\'S SPINE on the same pair (the Mekhilta on Exodus 20 unopened by any ledger: the scan). The middah: two verses of one law read as one saying (I13\'s kin — the contradiction resolved not by a third verse but by the one voice; the Sifrei\'s own formula), a REFERENCE the Sifrei makes between the two copies (LR1). THE SCHEMA QUESTION ON THE TABLE: this row is the tradition filing one commandment\'s two seats under one header — the fourth word\'s two copies as one utterance. Fresh in both files.')
srow(306, 16, 'MATERIAL', f'"MY DOCTRINE SHALL DROP AS THE RAIN" (32:2) — "MY DOCTRINE" IS NOTHING BUT WORDS OF TORAH, AS IT IS SAID "FOR I GIVE YOU A GOOD DOCTRINE, FORSAKE NOT MY TORAH" (PROVERBS 4:2); … "TAKE WITH YOU WORDS AND RETURN" (HOSEA 14:3) — "WORDS" ARE NOTHING BUT WORDS OF TORAH, AS IT IS SAID "THESE WORDS THE LORD SPOKE TO ALL YOUR ASSEMBLY" (5:22). The Song\'s row (piska 306 on 32:1, its sixteenth row; {SP_(306, 16, "אין", "דברים", "אלא", "דברי", "תורה")} "words are nothing but words of Torah"): 5:22\'s "THESE WORDS" made the lexicon entry for "words" wherever the Song and the prophet say it — the ten words the definition of the word "words" ("words of Torah" three times in the row in both files, computed); the row cites the export\'s 5:19 = THE DB\'S 5:22 (the map: the offset of three after the export\'s 18), "(דברים ה יט)" in the Hebrew and "(Dt.5:19)" in the English — the export\'s numbering in both. The middah: the word fixed from its defining seat (E7 — "words" from 5:22, the Sifrei\'s own "nothing but" formula; the same move 323:1 made for "this" from 4:44 at sitting 2). Fresh in both files.')
srow(357, 40, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND THERE AROSE NOT A PROPHET SINCE IN ISRAEL LIKE MOSES" (34:10) — IN ISRAEL NONE AROSE, BUT AMONG THE NATIONS ONE AROSE: BALAAM SON OF BEOR. BUT THERE IS A DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE PROPHECY OF MOSES AND THE PROPHECY OF BALAAM: MOSES DID NOT KNOW WHO SPOKE WITH HIM, BALAAM KNEW ("THE UTTERANCE OF HIM WHO HEARS THE WORDS OF GOD", NUMBERS 24:16); MOSES DID NOT KNOW WHEN HE WOULD SPEAK WITH HIM, BALAAM KNEW; MOSES SPOKE WITH HIM ONLY STANDING, AS IT IS SAID "AND YOU, HERE STAND WITH ME" (5:31), BALAAM FALLEN ("FALLEN DOWN AND HIS EYES OPEN", NUMBERS 24:4) — A PARABLE OF THE KING\'S COOK WHO KNOWS THE COST OF THE KING\'S TABLE. The book\'s last piska, its fortieth row (on 34:10; {SP_(357, 40, "אלא", "כשהוא", "עומד")} "only when he is standing"): 5:31\'s "STAND HERE WITH ME" read as the POSTURE of Moses\' prophecy — the third of three contrasts ("Moses did not" three times in the Hebrew row, computed), Balaam\'s fall the counter-seat; the row cites the export\'s 5:28 = THE DB\'S 5:31 (the map), "(דברים ה כח)" and "(Dt.5:28)". The middah: the contrast of two subjects by paired verses (E28\'s kin — the two set against each other seat by seat), each rung a citation; the closing parable (E26, the mashal of the king) on the cook who knows the cost. The ink beside it: "stand with me" one seat, "with me" twenty Torah seats (computed). Fresh in both files.')

